Chapter One:

Design principle: Identify the aspects of your application that very and separate them from
what stays the same. (Encapsulate parts that vary -> fewer unintended consequences and more flexible systems)

Design principle: Favor composition over inheritance.
Creating systems using composition gives you more flexibility and lets you change behavior at runtime

Design principle: Program to interfaces, not implementations
Instead of programming duck flying behavior directly to the Duck class, programming it to an interface to make
code more flexible and loosely coupled.
When you program to implementations: you bind your code to specific details; you make change hard; you reduce
flexibility; you hurt testability
When you program to an interface: you depend only on behavior; you can swap implementations; you improve testability;
reduce effects of change
